[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings this  women's history month our new series  changing the game is profiling  extraordinary women making an impact now  and for future generations this morning  we meet a leader in the fight for  domestic labor and care workers rights  you know more than 90 percent of those  roles are filled by women and more than  half of them by women of color i genpoos  work has drawn the attention of  billionaire philanthropist melinda  french gates whose company was behind  part of a 10 million dollar grant for  pu's group jamie yukis sat down with  both women at the los angeles non-profit  e-bell which has a mission to educate  and uplift women  everybody feels invisible and isolated  and struggling  because we haven't supported them and  that's the opportunity here for more  than two decades igenpoo has been  drawing attention to a so-called  invisible workforce as the executive  director of the national domestic  workers alliance she advocates for the  country's caregivers we represent the  two and a half million women who work  inside of our homes who make everything  else possible who says proper  recognition and compensation for the  home care aides nannies and house  cleaners are long overdue  what a child care worker an early  childhood educator produces  is human potential  what a home care worker produces  is dignity and quality of life for the  people who raised us  what could be more valuable  why is igen a game changer  oh my gosh igen is a game changer  because she has been advocating in this  caregiving space for so long and she's  really elevated it on the national stage  melinda french gates first met igenpoo  through the bill and melinda gates  foundation as a privileged businesswoman  philanthropist and mother she  immediately recognized the critical  nature of pooh's work we would be  talking to single moms in low income  settings and hearing their struggle you  know saying my gosh i used to rely on my  mom to take care of my children so i a  single mom can work now i'm taking care  of my mother and i don't know where to  put my children it hit the nail on the  head i thought oh my god this is  impossible for these women the pandemic  really put us all in a different kind of  crisis like our kids were home from  school struggling with online learning  our parents on lockdown in nursing homes  or thousands of miles away and we can be  doing everything right  and without the programs and policies  that support caregiving it's just not  enough why are we so far behind  well i think because so often in the  united states we think of the workforce  as kind of what it was like in the 1950s  where you know one person went off with  their briefcase to the office and the  woman stayed home  and yet today of all the kids in the u.s  25 have a single parent and of all  families 54 are dual income earners i  see you really nodding your head yeah i  mean i think we have this assumption  that care is kind of a personal  responsibility that  is to be managed mostly by the women in  our households right and if we can't  figure it out we consider it a personal  failure we actually need  policies systems programs a workforce  and i think it really starts with  investing in care programs child care  paid leave  home and community-based care for the  aging and our care workforce that has  been so undervalued i mean the average  annual income of a home care worker in  this country is eighteen thousand two  hundred dollars per year how is that  sustainable i mean imagine trying to  take care of your kids  so the people we count on to care for us  as their profession can't care for  themselves and their own families doing  this work these are also jobs in our  economy right some of the fastest  growing occupations in our entire  economy are child care workers and home  care workers there is a proposal called  the better care better jobs act that  would invest in the home care workforce  it would be the biggest investment in  the creation of good jobs for women and  women of color in the history of this  country  and it would be profound because this is  a group of workers that has never fully  been recognized as the professionals and  skilled workers that they are you'll be  a game changer well that's is there a  lesson here too that when women support  other women stuff gets done yes yes yes  [Laughter]  that was jamie yukis reporting

